export default {"id":"launchkey-level-5","number":5,"title":"System Configuration & Hardware Settings","skillBand":"Textbook","duration":"From the course textbook","summary":"The internal Settings engine of the Launchkey MK4 61 governs fundamental MIDI routing, channel segregation, dynamic velocity translation, and arpeggiator note sources. Mastering these configuration registers is essential for eliminating mul","modules":[{"id":"launchkey-m-5-1-1","code":"5.1","title":"System Configuration & Hardware Settings","blurb":"The internal Settings engine of the Launchkey MK4 61 governs fundamental MIDI routing, channel segregation, dynamic velocity translation, and arpeggiator note sources. Mastering these configuration registers is essential for eliminating mul","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-14","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 5.1 — MIDI Channel Routing & Dynamic Curves","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"The internal Settings engine of the Launchkey MK4 61 governs fundamental MIDI routing, channel segregation, dynamic velocity translation, and arpeggiator note sources. Mastering these configuration registers is essential for eliminating multi-timbral routing conflicts and ensuring seamless communication with multi-track DAW projects and external hardware synths."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Navigate the hardware Settings menu, execute parameter edits, and exit with state restoration.","Formulate the default factory MIDI channel assignments for Part A, Part B, Chords, and Drums.","Configure pad velocity response curves independently of keybed curves.","Detail the operational mechanics of the <code>Arp Velocity</code> toggle and the <code>Arp Note Source</code> register."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-5-1-1","title":"5.1.1: Settings Menu Navigation Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.1: Settings Menu Navigation Architecture","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Launchkey MK4 61 stores its operating parameters in internal EEPROM memory accessed via the <code>Settings</code> button:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|                     SETTINGS MENU NAVIGATION                            |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+\n| Hardware Control  | Navigation Action                                   |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+\n| [ Settings ]      | Enters Settings menu; second press exits &amp; restores |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+\n| [ Pad Bank ▲ / ▼ ]| Cycles sequentially through Settings parameter pages|\n+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+\n| [ Track ◄ / ► ]   | Decrements / Increments parameter value             |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+\n| [ Encoder 1 ]     | Continuous rotary parameter adjustment              |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+\n| [ Pads 1–16 ]     | Direct selection for discrete enumerated lists      |\n+-------------------+-----------------------------------------------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<em>State Restoration</em>: Upon pressing <code>Settings</code> to exit, the controller automatically restores the pad, encoder, and fader modes that were active prior to opening the menu."}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-5-1-2","title":"5.1.2: Authoritative Factory MIDI Channel Defaults","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.2: Authoritative Factory MIDI Channel Defaults","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"To prevent channel crosstalk when playing keys, chords, and drum pads simultaneously, the Launchkey routes distinct performance engines to dedicated default MIDI channels:"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">+-------------------------------------------------------------------------+\n|              AUTHORITATIVE FACTORY DEFAULT MIDI CHANNELS                |\n+---------------------+-----------------+---------------+-----------------+\n| Functional Subsystem| Default Channel | Valid Range   | Settings Name   |\n+---------------------+-----------------+---------------+-----------------+\n| Keybed (Part A)     | **Channel 1**   | Ch 1 – 16     | \"Part A Ch\"     |\n| Keybed (Part B)     | **Channel 2**   | Ch 1 – 16     | \"Part B Ch\"     |\n| Chord Engines       | **Channel 3**   | Ch 1 – 16     | \"Chords Ch\"     |\n| Drum Pad Mode       | **Channel 10**  | Ch 1 – 16     | \"Drums Ch\"      |\n+---------------------+-----------------+---------------+-----------------+</pre>"},{"type":"prose","html":"<pre style=\"white-space:pre-wrap;font-family:ui-monospace,monospace\">       SIGNAL FLOW: MULTI-CHANNEL SEGREGATION\n+───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────+\n| Keybed (Zone A)  ──► MIDI Channel 1 ──► DAW Track 1 (Lead Syn)|\n| Keybed (Zone B)  ──► MIDI Channel 2 ──► DAW Track 2 (Bass Syn)|\n| Chord Map Engine ──► MIDI Channel 3 ──► DAW Track 3 (Pad Syn) |\n| Drum Pad Matrix  ──► MIDI Channel 10 ─► DAW Track 4 (Drum Rack|\n+───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────+</pre>"}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-5-1-3","title":"5.1.3: Independent Pad Velocity Curves","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.3: Independent Pad Velocity Curves","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Pad strike dynamics are decoupled from keybed dynamics. In Settings -&gt; <code>Vel Curve (Pads)</code>:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Soft</strong>: Highly sensitive; triggers maximum velocity (127) with light taps.","<strong>Normal (Default)</strong>: Linear, natural velocity scaling.","<strong>Hard</strong>: Requires aggressive pad strikes for high velocities.","<strong>Fixed</strong>: All pad strikes output the value defined in <code>Fixed Velocity</code> (default 120)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-5-1-4","title":"5.1.4: Arpeggiator Dynamics & Note Source Routing","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.1.4: Arpeggiator Dynamics & Note Source Routing","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"Settings provides two dedicated registers controlling how the internal Arpeggiator interacts with the keybed:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>Arp Velocity</code> (On / Off — Default: On)</strong>:","<em>On</em>: Arpeggiated notes inherit the exact physical strike velocity of the keys held on the keybed.","<em>Off</em>: Arpeggiated notes ignore key velocity and output at a standardized baseline velocity of <strong>100</strong> (or the accented value when Accent is triggered).","<strong><code>Arp Note Source</code> (Part A / Part B — Default: Part A)</strong>:","Selects which keyboard zone feeds the arpeggiator engine when operating in Split or Multi-Part mode (Module 11)."],"ordered":true}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Settings navigation protocol; authoritative default channels (Part A = 1, Part B = 2, Chords = 3, Drums = 10); independent pad velocity curves; Arp Velocity (On vs. Off = 100); Arp Note Source (Part A vs Part B).","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Speculation regarding channel 0 or channels &gt; 16; changing channels without documenting the baseline restore protocol."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Practical Guided Task & Assessment Checkpoint","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Task</strong>: Open Settings, inspect the channels for Part A, Part B, Chords, and Drums. Change <code>Chords Ch</code> to Channel 1, verify in a MIDI monitor that Chord Map outputs on Channel 1, and then restore <code>Chords Ch</code> to Channel 3."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Checkpoint Validation</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["Settings navigation executed correctly.","Factory default channels verified and restored: Part A (1), Part B (2), Chords (3), Drums (10)."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]},{"id":"launchkey-m-5-2-2","code":"5.2","title":"System Configuration & Hardware Settings","blurb":"This module details the complete hardware environment settings of the Launchkey MK4 61, including pad aftertouch pressure protocols, external MIDI clock distribution, optical telemetry (LED and OLED brightness), rotary encoder acceleration,","outcomes":[],"sources":[],"practiceFocus":[],"checkpoints":[],"lessons":[{"id":"launchkey-l-o-15","title":"Section overview","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"Section 5.2 — Aftertouch, MIDI Clock, Hardware Feedback & Startup State","blocks":[{"type":"heading","text":"Module Overview","level":3},{"type":"prose","html":"This module details the complete hardware environment settings of the Launchkey MK4 61, including pad aftertouch pressure protocols, external MIDI clock distribution, optical telemetry (LED and OLED brightness), rotary encoder acceleration, fader pickup modes, external MIDI feedback, Vegas demonstration mode, and non-volatile startup configuration."},{"type":"heading","text":"Learning Objectives","level":3},{"type":"list","items":["Formulate the operational differences between Polyphonic Aftertouch, Channel Aftertouch, and Disabled Aftertouch on the pad matrix.","Configure pad pressure response thresholds (Low, Normal, High).","Detail the operational role of the <code>MIDI Clock Out</code> register.","Adjust optical brightness parameters for studio versus stage environments and configure display sleep timeouts.","Define the scope of the <code>Encoders Curve</code> parameter and formulate why it does not affect DAW plugin control.","Contrast <code>Jump</code> versus <code>Pickup</code> fader takeover behaviors.","Formulate the function of <code>External Feedback</code> and the diagnostic recovery for <code>Vegas Mode</code>.","Complete Assessment Gate D."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-5-2-1","title":"5.2.1: Pad Aftertouch & Pressure Thresholds","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.1: Pad Aftertouch & Pressure Thresholds","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The 16 RGB pads incorporate patented continuous pressure sensors:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>Pad Aftertouch</code> Settings Register</strong>:","<strong>Polyphonic (Factory Default)</strong>: Each individual pad transmits independent polyphonic key pressure messages (<code>0xA0</code>–<code>0xAF</code>, Data Byte 1 = Note Number, Data Byte 2 = Pressure 0–127). Allows modulating one note's filter while holding others static.","<strong>Channel</strong>: Pressure from any pad is converted into global Channel Pressure (<code>0xD0</code>–<code>0xDF</code>, Data Byte 1 = Pressure 0–127) applied uniformly across the entire channel.","<strong>Off</strong>: Continuous pressure sensing is disabled; pads transmit Note On/Off velocity events only.","<strong><code>Pad AT Threshold</code></strong>:","<strong>Low</strong>: Engages aftertouch with minimal resting finger pressure.","<strong>Normal (Default)</strong>: Balanced pressure threshold.","<strong>High</strong>: Requires deliberate, forceful downward pressure to engage aftertouch (prevents accidental modulation during rapid drumming)."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-5-2-2","title":"5.2.2: MIDI Clock Output & Sync Generation","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.2: MIDI Clock Output & Sync Generation","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Setting</strong>: <code>MIDI Clock Out</code> (On / Off — Factory Default: <strong>On</strong>).","<strong>Function</strong>: When set to <strong>On</strong>, the Launchkey transmits real-time MIDI Clock packets (<code>0xF8</code>), MIDI Start (<code>0xFA</code>), and MIDI Stop (<code>0xFC</code>) out of the 5-pin DIN output and USB MIDI Interface 1 whenever its internal arpeggiator is running.","<strong>Clock Slaving</strong>: When external MIDI Clock is received from a host DAW via USB, the Launchkey automatically slaves its internal clock to the incoming tempo, and <code>MIDI Clock Out</code> acts as a clock distributor to external synths."],"ordered":false}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-5-2-3","title":"5.2.3: Optical Telemetry: Brightness & Display Timeout","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.3: Optical Telemetry: Brightness & Display Timeout","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>LED Brightness</code></strong>: Numerical range <strong>1 to 10</strong> (Factory Default = <strong>8</strong>). Adjusts pad and button backlight intensity for dark studio control rooms versus high-ambient stage lighting.","<strong><code>Screen Brightness</code></strong>: Numerical range <strong>1 to 10</strong> (Factory Default = <strong>8</strong>). Adjusts OLED display contrast.","<strong><code>Display Timeout</code></strong>: Numerical range <strong>1 to 10 seconds</strong> (Factory Default = <strong>5 seconds</strong>). Sets how long temporary parameter popups remain visible before returning to the primary view."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-5-2-4","title":"5.2.4: Encoder Curves & Fader Takeover Mechanics","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.4: Encoder Curves & Fader Takeover Mechanics","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>Encoders Curve</code> (Slow / Medium / Fast — Default: Medium)</strong>:","Sets rotary acceleration for the 8 continuous encoders.","<em>CRITICAL SCOPE RULE</em>: This setting applies <strong>strictly to Custom Modes 1–4</strong>. It has <strong>zero effect on native DAW Plugin / Mixer / Sends / Transport encoder modes</strong>, which follow host-defined acceleration tables.","<strong><code>Fader Pickup Type</code> (Jump / Pickup — Default: Jump)</strong>:","<strong>Jump (Default)</strong>: Software parameter immediately snaps to the physical fader position the instant the fader is moved.","<strong>Pickup</strong>: Software parameter remains unchanged until the physical fader travels across and intersects the current software value, eliminating sudden volume jumps."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-5-2-5","title":"5.2.5: External Feedback & Vegas Mode Diagnostics","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.5: External Feedback & Vegas Mode Diagnostics","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong><code>External Feedback</code> (On / Off — Default: On)</strong>:","When set to <strong>On</strong>, incoming MIDI note data received by the Launchkey illuminates corresponding drum pads or custom mode pads. Essential for visual clip monitoring and step sequence visual feedback.","<strong><code>Vegas Mode</code> (On / Off — Default: Off)</strong>:","When enabled, the Launchkey initiates a rainbow animation across all RGB pads and buttons after several minutes of inactivity.","<em>Studio Practice</em>: Ensure Vegas Mode is set to <strong>Off</strong> in studio environments to prevent visual distraction. If a unit powers on with flashing lights, open Settings and disable Vegas Mode."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8},{"id":"launchkey-l-5-2-6","title":"5.2.6: Non-Volatile Power-On Modes","blocks":[{"type":"step","title":"5.2.6: Non-Volatile Power-On Modes","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"The Launchkey MK4 61 allows defining its default operational state upon USB power-on:"},{"type":"list","items":["<strong>Factory Default Startup State</strong>:","Faders: <strong>Custom 1</strong>","Encoders: <strong>Custom 1</strong>","Pads: <strong>Drum Mode</strong>","Custom startup configurations can be stored in EEPROM via Settings -&gt; <code>Power On Modes</code>."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Comprehensive Settings Reference Table","blocks":[{"type":"table","headers":["Parameter Name","Factory Default","Permitted Range / Values","Operational Scope"],"rows":[["<strong>Part A Ch</strong>","<strong>1</strong>","1 – 16","Keybed Part A MIDI Channel"],["<strong>Part B Ch</strong>","<strong>2</strong>","1 – 16","Keybed Part B MIDI Channel"],["<strong>Chords Ch</strong>","<strong>3</strong>","1 – 16","Chord Modes MIDI Channel"],["<strong>Drums Ch</strong>","<strong>10</strong>","1 – 16","Drum Mode MIDI Channel"],["<strong>Vel Curve (Keys)</strong>","<strong>Normal</strong>","Soft, Normal, Hard, Fixed","Keybed dynamic response"],["<strong>Vel Curve (Pads)</strong>","<strong>Normal</strong>","Soft, Normal, Hard, Fixed","Pad dynamic response"],["<strong>Fixed Velocity</strong>","<strong>120</strong>","1 – 127","Fixed velocity value / Arp accent"],["<strong>Arp Velocity</strong>","<strong>On</strong>","On, Off (Fixed 100)","Arp note dynamic velocity"],["<strong>Arp Note Source</strong>","<strong>Part A</strong>","Part A, Part B","Arp keybed input zone"],["<strong>Pad Aftertouch</strong>","<strong>Polyphonic</strong>","Off, Channel, Polyphonic","Pad continuous pressure output"],["<strong>Pad AT Threshold</strong>","<strong>Normal</strong>","Low, Normal, High","Aftertouch engagement point"],["<strong>MIDI Clock Out</strong>","<strong>On</strong>","On, Off","5-pin DIN / USB clock broadcast"],["<strong>LED Brightness</strong>","<strong>8</strong>","1 – 10","Surface backlighting intensity"],["<strong>Screen Brightness</strong>","<strong>8</strong>","1 – 10","OLED display contrast"],["<strong>Display Timeout</strong>","<strong>5 s</strong>","1 – 10 seconds","Parameter overlay duration"],["<strong>Encoders Curve</strong>","<strong>Medium</strong>","Slow, Medium, Fast","<strong>Custom Modes only</strong> acceleration"],["<strong>Fader Pickup Type</strong>","<strong>Jump</strong>","Jump, Pickup","Linear fader parameter takeover"],["<strong>External Feedback</strong>","<strong>On</strong>","On, Off","Incoming MIDI pad illumination"],["<strong>Vegas Mode</strong>","<strong>Off</strong>","On, Off","Inactivity demo light animation"],["<strong>Power On Modes</strong>","<strong>Cust1/Cust1/Drum</strong>","Legal hardware modes","Default hardware power-up state"]],"caption":null}]},{"type":"step","title":"Operational Rules Summary","blocks":[{"type":"list","items":["<strong>mustCover</strong>: Full Settings table; Polyphonic aftertouch default vs Channel/Off; AT threshold; MIDI clock out; brightness (1–10) and display timeout (1–10s); Encoder curve affects Custom Modes only; Fader Jump vs. Pickup; External feedback; Vegas mode disable procedure; power-on defaults.","<strong>mustNotCover</strong>: Invention of undocumented Settings menu pages."],"ordered":false}]},{"type":"step","title":"Assessment Gate D (Hardware Settings Mastery)","blocks":[{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Objective</strong>: Prove complete mastery of internal configuration registers and baseline parameter restoration."},{"type":"prose","html":"<strong>Self-Assessment Checklist</strong>:"},{"type":"list","items":["[ ] Enter Settings, navigate to <code>Part A Ch</code>, <code>Part B Ch</code>, <code>Chords Ch</code>, and <code>Drums Ch</code>, and verify values match <code>1</code>, <code>2</code>, <code>3</code>, <code>10</code>.","[ ] Change <code>Pad Aftertouch</code> from <code>Polyphonic</code> to <code>Channel</code>, test in a DAW MIDI monitor, and restore to <code>Polyphonic</code>.","[ ] Set <code>Display Timeout</code> to <code>2</code> seconds, test parameter preview, and restore to <code>5</code> seconds.","[ ] Explain why changing <code>Encoders Curve</code> to <code>Fast</code> does not change rotary behavior when controlling an Ableton Live native plugin.","[ ] Successfully exit Settings and confirm previous surface modes are restored."],"ordered":true}]}],"minutes":8}],"exercises":[]}]};
